--TEST--
`Core\Db\Connection`'s two readonly members answer the connection and not the block that named it: `driver()` is the `Core\Db\Driver` case the `[db.<name>]` block wrote, and `isOpen()` is `true` right up to the `close` that ends it — `rule:core-classes/db-connection-is-named` and `rule:security/db-pool-reset-is-a-boundary` and spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Driver
// SQLite needs no server, so this is a connection that really opened — which
// is what makes `driver()` a fact read off the thing that was opened rather
// than off the configuration key. `db-a-sqlite-block-opens-a-file-and-runs-
// statements-over-it.nvst` is the case that argues why that distinction is
// worth a running database.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

// § 18's `driver()`. The answer is the enum and not its name: a case is a
// value (`rule:enums/closed-integer-type`), so a program compares rather than parses.
echo "driver ", $db->driver() == Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite ? "is sqlite" : "is something else", "\n";

// It is the connection's own driver, so it does not change under it. Asking
// twice is asked here because the value is read out of the connection each
// time rather than cached in the handle.
echo "still ", $db->driver() == Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite ? "sqlite" : "something else", "\n";

// § 18's `isOpen()`, before and after. It is bookkeeping about the request's
// own table and never a round trip, which is why a program may write it in a
// condition.
echo "open before close: ", $db->isOpen() ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// `rule:security/db-pool-reset-is-a-boundary`'s release, reached early: the connection goes back to this
// core's pool here instead of at the end of the request.
$db->close();

echo "open after close: ", $db->isOpen() ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: a SQLite connection's file path comes from its config block, so
# `db.connect` covers it and there is no address to grant.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
driver is sqlite
still sqlite
open before close: yes
open after close: no
